.TH "openvas\-nasl" "1" "October 2026" "23.51.0" "User commands"
.SH NAME
openvas\-nasl \- OpenVAS standalone NASL interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]openvas\-nasl\f[R] [\f[I]option\f[R]\&...]
\f[I]file\f[R]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]openvas\-nasl\f[R] executes a set of NASL scripts against a given
target host.
It can also be used to determine if a NASL script has any syntax errors
by running it in parse (\f[B]\-p\f[R]) or lint (\f[B]\-L\f[R]) mode.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-T\f[R] \f[I]tracefile\f[R], \f[B]\[en]trace\f[R] \f[I]tracefile\f[R]
Make nasl write verbosely what the script does in the file
\f[I]tracefile\f[R] , ala \[aq]set \-x\[aq] under sh, pass `\-' to send
the output to stderr.
.TP
\f[B]\-t\f[R] \f[I]target\f[R], \f[B]\[en]target\f[R] \f[I]target\f[R]
Apply the NASL script to \f[I]target\f[R] which may be a single host
(127.0.0.1), a whole subnet (192.168.1.0/24) or several subnets
(192.168.1.0/24, 192.168.243.0/24).
.TP
\f[B]\-e\f[R] \f[I]iface\f[R], \f[B]\[en]source\-iface\f[R] \f[I]iface\f[R]
Specify the network interface to be used as the source for established
connections.
.TP
\f[B]\-s\f[R], \f[B]\[en]safe\f[R]
Set the return value of safe_checks() to 1.
(See the OpenVAS Scanner documentation to know what the safe checks are)
Implies \-B.
.TP
\f[B]\-D\f[R], \f[B]\[en]description\f[R]
Only run the description part of the script.
.TP
\f[B]\-B\f[R], \f[B]\[en]both\f[R]
Run in description mode before running the script.
.TP
\f[B]\-p\f[R], \f[B]\[en]parse\f[R]
Only parse the script, don\[cq]t execute it.
.TP
\f[B]\-L\f[R], \f[B]\[en]lint\f[R]
\f[B]Lint\f[R] the script (run extended checks).
.TP
\f[B]\-X\f[R], \f[B]\[en]disable\-signing\f[R]
Run the script with disabled signature verification.
.TP
\f[B]\-i\f[R] \f[I]dir\f[R], \f[B]\[en]include\-dir\f[R] \f[I]dir\f[R]
Search for includes in \f[I]dir\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\[en]help\f[R]
Show help.
.TP
\f[B]\-V\f[R], \f[B]\[en]version\f[R]
Show the version of NASL.
.TP
\f[B]\-d\f[R], \f[B]\[en]debug\f[R]
Output debug information to stderr.
.TP
\f[B]\-r\f[R] \f[I]port\-range\f[R], \f[B]\[en]port\-range\f[R] \f[I]port\-range\f[R]
This is the default range of ports that the scanner plugins will probe.
The syntax of this option is flexible, it can be a single range
(\[dq]1\-1500\[dq]), several ports (\[dq]21,23,80\[dq]), several ranges
of ports (\[dq]1\-1500,32000\-33000\[dq]).
Note that you can specify UDP and TCP ports by prefixing each range by T
or U. For instance, the following range will make openvas scan UDP ports
1 to 1024 and TCP ports 1 to 65535 : \[dq]T:1\-65535,U:1\-1024\[dq].
.TP
\f[B]\-k\f[R] \f[I]key=value\f[R], \f[B]\[en]kb\f[R] \f[I]key=value\f[R]
Set KB \f[I]key\f[R] to \f[I]value\f[R].
Can be used multiple times.
.SH SEE ALSO
\f[B]openvas(8)\f[R], \f[B]openvas\-nasl\-lint(1)\f[R]
.SH HISTORY
NASL comes from a private project called \[aq]pkt_forge\[aq], which was
written in late 1998 by Renaud Deraison and which was an interactive
shell to forge and send raw IP packets (this pre\-dates Perl\[aq]s
Net::RawIP by a couple of weeks).
It was then extended to do a wide range of network\-related operations
and integrated into the scanner as \[aq]NASL\[aq].
.PP
The parser was completely hand\-written and a pain to work with.
In Mid\-2002, Michel Arboi wrote a bison parser for NASL, and he and
Renaud Deraison re\-wrote NASL from scratch.
Although the \[dq]new\[dq] NASL was nearly working as early as August
2002, Michel\[aq]s laziness made us wait for early 2003 to have it
working completely.
.PP
After the original authors decided to stop the Open Source development
in 2005, most changes and maintenance works were done by Greenbone
Networks.
.SH AUTHOR
Most of the engine is (C) 2003 Michel Arboi, most of the built\-in
functions are (C) 2003 Renaud Deraison.
Most new code since 2005 developed by Greenbone AG.
